# TMT design principles

Colour and text rules for everything TMT draws in the terminal. Git-ignored: this is a
working reference for whoever is building the interface, not shipped documentation.

The values below are not aspirational. They are the ones in `agent_ui.py`, and that
module is the only place they are defined. If a rule here and the code disagree, the
code is right and this file is stale.

## The gradient

TMT has one visual idea: a gradient running **red → orange → green**. It is the
progress bar, the frames, the logo, the spinner. Nothing else needs inventing.

Six stops, interpolated (`agent_ui._gradient`):

| Position | RGB | Hex | Reads as |
|---|---|---|---|
| 10 | `220, 38, 38` | `#DC2626` | red |
| 40 | `249, 115, 22` | `#F97316` | orange |
| 60 | `234, 179, 8` | `#EAB308` | amber |
| 80 | `132, 204, 22` | `#84CC16` | lime |
| 95 | `34, 197, 94` | `#22C55E` | green |
| 100 | `74, 222, 128` | `#4ADE80` | light green |

One neutral, and only one: `DIM = 88, 88, 88` (`#585858`), for the unfilled part of a
bar and for detail that must recede.

There is no other colour. No blue, no magenta, no per-feature palette. A second colour
system would make two things look unrelated that are the same thing.

## What the gradient means

Position in the gradient carries meaning, so do not pick a colour for looks:

- **Red is early, green is done.** `_gradient(progress)` maps 0–100 directly, so a
  progress bar's colour and its number always agree.
- **Ambient surfaces cycle** rather than sit still: `gradient_at(phase)` walks
  red→green over the first half of `GRADIENT_CYCLE` (2.4s) and back over the second, so
  it can loop forever without snapping from green to red. Use it for anything that is
  alive but not measuring progress — the THINKING word, the logo, a menu that is
  waiting to be driven.
- **`spread`** in `cycle_text` decides how much of the gradient one string covers. `1.0`
  spans the whole range across the text; smaller values keep a string closer to one hue.
  Paired elements (a left and right frame edge) use the same phase offset by `0.5` so
  they differ without looking unrelated.
- **A fixed phase for anything printed once.** `agent_menu.BRAND_PHASE` /
  `BRAND_SPREAD` are where the non-animating gradient starts and how far it
  travels. A line printed into scrollback is read, not watched, and a wordmark
  that is a different colour every launch is not a wordmark. The span starts
  past red on purpose: red is the error end of the scale.

## What does not carry the gradient

The gradient marks what is alive or measuring. Three surfaces are deliberately
plain — the terminal's own colour, no cycle, no phase:

| Surface | Why |
|---|---|
| the prompt box: rules, marker, typed text | it is being typed into. Colouring it says "this is progressing", which it is not, and an animated box repaints under the caret |
| the streaming reply box | it holds the answer as it arrives, and a reader is reading it |
| the finished answer from `render_response` | the one thing on screen the user came for. A coloured border made it look like another instrument |

Plain is not only a matter of taste here: two frames of an undecorated box are
byte-identical, so `LiveRegion` skips the repaint, and a repaint skipped is a
caret that does not move. The flicker fix and this rule are the same fix.

### The one exception: capability commands in the field

`/plan`, `/review` and `/verify` in the input field carry the gradient
(`agent_menu.paint_capabilities`). It is the only coloured thing on the typed
surface and it is worth being precise about why it does not break the rule
above.

**It is not the typed text, it is an instrument that happens to sit in it.** A
capability command is a switch the user is throwing: it decides whether TMT may
plan, whether a second agent will audit the work, and whether the final answer
will be gated. Everything else in the field is prose the model will read. The
gradient marks the two apart, and marking them apart is exactly what the
gradient is for.

**It is a FIXED phase — `CAPABILITY_PHASE = 0.0`, `CAPABILITY_SPREAD = 0.5` —
so the mechanical half of the rule is untouched.** The clock is never
consulted, the token is the same colour in every frame, two frames of an
untouched box are still byte-identical, `LiveRegion` still skips the repaint
and the caret still does not move. This is the "fixed phase for anything
printed once" rule above, applied to something drawn repeatedly but never
changing. An ANIMATED highlight here would put the flicker straight back, on
the one surface where it is least tolerable, and is the thing not to do.

**It starts at red on purpose**, which is the other departure: `BRAND_PHASE`
begins past red because a header is not an error. Here the full red → orange →
green run is the point — it is what was asked for, and a switch being thrown
reads as a scale rather than as a hue.

Colour is still never the message. Strip the escapes and the row says
`/plan`, which is the command spelled out; on a terminal with ANSI but no
colour the token takes `BOLD + UNDERLINE` instead, and in a piped run it takes
nothing at all.

## The neutral ramp, and the one strip that uses it

There is one neutral for detail that recedes — `DIM` — and there is one other
grey, which is a **ramp** rather than a colour: `agent_panel.NEUTRAL_STEPS`,
black through grey to white, and it belongs to the bars of background agents
and to nothing else.

It is not a second palette. The gradient means *"the main agent is working and
this is how far along it is"*, and painting a worker's bar with it says the
same sentence about a different thing on the row below — at a glance five
gradient bars read as one process reported five times. So the delegated work
gets **the absence of a colour** rather than a colour of its own, which is
"one gradient, one neutral" spent on its most useful distinction. The rows
still read with the escapes stripped: each one says which agent it is and what
state it is in, in words.

### A delegation's contract on the row and on the card

A background agent spawned with constraints — read-only, a deadline, a report
it owes — says so on its own row and on its own card, and it says it in **the
absence of a colour like everything else in that strip**. Nothing here took a
position on the gradient: `READ ONLY` is a word, the countdown is a pair of
numbers, and both read exactly the same with the escapes stripped, which is
what the rule requires of a fact that changes what the user should expect from
a process they can see.

Three sizes of the same fact, and each is the size its surface has room for:

    ██░░░░░░ #3  RO  8:32/10:00  +0 -0  ~4k out  1m28s  running   <- the strip
    > #3: ~4k T +180                                              <- the card
       RO  8:32/10:00  F D S
    #3: ~4k T running                                             <- /agents
       READ ONLY  TIMEOUT 10:00  (8:30 left)
       REPORTS FILES  DIFF  SUMMARY

The strip and the card abbreviate because they are twenty to thirty columns
wide; `/agents` prints to the permanent surface with the width of the
terminal, so it is where the contract is actually *read*, and shrinking it
there to match the card would be abbreviating the one readout with room.

**The countdown is real, and that is the whole reason it is allowed.** A card
draws no elapsed time — the rule two sections down, and it stands — because a
duration that the repaint could not keep current would be a stale number
presented as a live one. What is drawn here is `remaining/limit`, read off the
same deadline arithmetic the runtime will actually stop the work with, so the
number on screen cannot drift from the number that matters; and it stops being
a countdown the moment the delegation does, showing the limit alone. A fake
countdown would be worse than none.

**A delegation that timed out is not painted as one that failed.** It takes
the `KILLED_POSITION` a stopped agent takes, and the word `timeout` rather
than `failed`. The two are separate states because a worker that ran out of
time has not crashed, and giving them one colour would undo on screen the
distinction the whole feature exists to draw.

**The counter says `4/10`, not `4`.** A count on its own answers "how much is
running" and not "how much more can". The maximum is asked of the register
rather than written into the interface, so the number promised on screen is
the number the capacity check enforces.

**An unconstrained delegation draws exactly what it drew before.** No chip
row, no countdown, two rows on the card. The contract is the only thing that
adds anything, which is the same rule the plan column and the capabilities row
follow: a session that does not use a feature must not be able to tell it
exists.

### The reviewbot strip

The independent reviewer draws in that same strip, under the main progress
bar, because it *is* one of those agents — same loop, same record, same
background thread. It is drawn `reviewbot` rather than `#4` because it is not
a numbered member of the fleet: there is one of it, it lives in its own slot,
and a number would send the reader looking for it in the AGENTS panel, where
it is not.

    ███░░░░░ reviewbot  2/6  ~12k T +840  47s   <- the instrument
      Read Lines agent_review.py                <- the current task, dim
      A1 ✓ Understand what the user asked for   ┐
      A2 ✓ Read the diff end to end             │ the reviewer's own agenda,
      A3 ● Check the tests cover the new paths  │ windowed on the item in hand
      A4 ○ Check every requirement              ┘

- **The bar is the neutral ramp, and turns red at exactly one moment**: when
  the review has come back blocking, or has not come back at all — the two
  states the runtime will not release a final answer from. Red is position 10,
  which is where `error` already sits, so nothing here added a colour. A stale
  pass is deliberately *not* red: the work moved under a review that really
  did pass, which is news about the code rather than news from the reviewer.
- **It is the one completion figure in TMT that is not a guess.** Every other
  bar shows budget spent, because nothing can know how close a process is to
  finishing. Here the reviewer itself declared how many things it would check
  and has itself reported which are done, so `2/6` is a fact it stated. An
  agenda with nothing in it is 0 and never 100.
- **The list is the reviewer's, and the runtime never supplies one.** A
  reviewer that declares nothing gets the bar and the activity row and no
  list at all. A default agenda would be a description of what a review is
  supposed to do, drawn beside a review that might be doing something else,
  and nothing on screen would tell the two apart. That is "never fabricate"
  on the one display whose whole job is to say what is actually happening.
- **`skipped` is its own state, with its reason on the row.** Collapsing it
  into `done` would let the strip claim coverage the review did not have, and
  a reviewer that could not check something must be able to say so rather than
  choose between a tick and a stuck list.
- **Four marks, distinct with the escapes stripped**, and the ASCII table is
  asked for whenever the stream cannot carry them — `✓ ● ○ –` becoming
  `+ > . -`. A terminal that draws a box rule but not a tick would otherwise
  put a replacement character on the one column being scanned.
- **Nothing on it animates.** Two frames of an unchanged strip are identical,
  which is what lets `LiveRegion` skip the repaint — the same bargain the
  prompt box strikes, and for the same reason.

## Text rules

- **Never colour alone.** Selection, state and errors must be legible with ANSI
  stripped. A `>` marker, a word, or a position — colour is confirmation, not the
  message. `_supports_color()` returns False more often than you would think: pipes,
  redirects, `NO_COLOR`.
- **Measure, never count.** Width is `display_width()`, not `len()`. Full-width and
  combining characters break every layout that counts characters, and have already
  done so twice in this project.
- **Leave a spare column.** Draw to `columns - 1`. Terminals disagree about what a row
  filled to the last column does, and the ones that auto-wrap silently cost a line the
  repaint arithmetic does not know about.
- **Degrade on purpose.** When `plain_output()` is True the stream cannot encode the
  decorative glyphs: use the ASCII set (`#`/`-` for bars, `+`/`|` for frames, `*` for
  the spinner) rather than emitting replacement marks. A deliberate ASCII interface
  reads as a choice; a row of `?` reads as a bug.
- **Write through `safe_write()`.** Decoration must never end the run.
- **Wrap prose on words, never at the column.** `wrap_words()` for anything a person
  reads as a sentence; `wrap_lines()` only where the columns mean something — a table
  laid out by hand, a line of code, a path. A word sawn in half at the edge of the
  terminal is the reader doing the renderer's job. The one exception is a word wider
  than a whole row, which is cut because moving it cannot help.
- **Generated text is markdown, and it is rendered.** Models write markdown whether or
  not anything reads it, so `agent_markdown` turns it into something meant for a
  person: the answer box, the streaming reply box and every progress line. The subset
  is GitHub's — headings, lists, task lists, quotes, rules, fenced code, pipe tables
  and every inline mark. The block grammar is for the two boxes; a line of the event
  stream gets the inline marks only, because it is drawn inside a row that already has
  a marker and an indent in front of it.
- **Markdown carries WEIGHT, and two marks are lit — in the reply, and only there.**
  Italic, strike and the one neutral are weights and nothing more. Bold is `BOLD + LIME`
  — the gradient's own lime stop at 80, `(132, 204, 22)`, muted a fifth of the way toward
  `DIM` — so the word a model chose to stress is the word the eye lands on. It is a
  **position on the existing ramp**, not a hue of its own.
- **The event stream takes the weights and none of the colour.** `render` draws a reply,
  which is a surface with nothing else on it, so the two lit marks are the only colour in
  the row. `render_message` draws one line of the event stream — a progress line, a
  milestone, a file edit — and that row already carries a gradient position on its
  **marker**, saying what kind of thing happened. A lime word and a cyan path inside it
  are a second colour system in the same row, one talking about the sentence while the
  other talks about the event; and on a progress line, the quietest thing TMT draws, they
  are a lit word inside something whose whole job is to recede. Neither mark loses
  anything by it: bold keeps its weight, code keeps its backticks.
- **A span inside a coloured row gives the row back.** `RESET` ends the caller's styling
  as well as the span's own, so a dim progress line carrying one `` `path` `` was dim as
  far as the first backtick and full brightness for the rest of the sentence — half quiet
  and half loud, and the loud half whatever the model happened to write after its first
  inline mark. Every span closes with `RESET` and then re-opens the base it interrupted.
  A row that opened nothing has nothing to put back and emits nothing.
- **Inline code is `CYAN` `(79, 209, 197)`, and it is the one colour in TMT that is off
  the ramp.** That is deliberate and it is the reason it is allowed. The ramp colours a
  **quantity** — how far along, how urgent, how done — and a path, a flag or a symbol in
  the middle of a sentence is not a quantity and has no position to take. It is a
  different **kind** of text, which is the whole reason to mark it, and a ramp position
  would say something false about it. Warm rather than blue, so it reads as a neighbour
  of the palette rather than as a second one.
- **Each lit mark keeps something that survives the colour being taken away.** Bold
  keeps its weight; inline code keeps its backticks. That is what lets both obey the
  rule every rendered row obeys — it still reads with the escapes stripped — and it is
  why the backticks stay even now that the span is coloured.
- **The lime never animates, and is never the colour alone.** No clock is consulted, so
  two frames of an unchanged reply are still byte-identical, `LiveRegion` still skips
  the repaint and the caret still does not move — the same fixed-value answer the
  capability commands in the input field give, for the same mechanical reason. And it
  is always emitted with `BOLD`, so a terminal that refuses colour still shows the
  emphasis as weight. Colour marks the emphasis; it is never what the emphasis *is*.
- **Only a level-1 heading is ruled.** A document has one title, and a rule under every
  `###` in a long reply draws more lines than text.

## Voice

- Plain, factual, lower-case-ish. No exclamation marks, no emoji, no marketing.
- Say what happened, not how it felt. "Nothing was committed" beats "Oops".
- An error names the thing to do next. If there is nothing to do next, say that too.
- Short lines. The terminal is narrow and the text is read once.

## Layout

- One idea per screen. The startup screen is a logo, a few facts, a divider, a menu.
- Dividers are a single rule, full width minus one. On a menu it is in the gradient;
  on the prompt box it is plain, for the reason in the table above.
- **One rule per boundary.** Two rules a blank line apart are not a boundary, they
  are a box with nothing in it. The session header used to end in a rule and the
  prompt box below it drew its own, and the screen opened with an empty frame
  between them. A rule belongs to the thing it encloses; the header has none.
- **Fill the window.** `_content_width` is `columns - 1` with a floor and no ceiling.
  It used to stop at 72, which on a wide terminal left the session in a strip down
  the left and made the box read as a panel dropped onto the terminal rather than
  as the interface of the thing running in it.
- **A screen opens at the top and reads downward.** `clear_screen` erases the viewport
  and homes the cursor as the startup menu opens and again as the session behind it
  does, so each begins where a reader begins. The header is the first thing on the
  window; the question, the work and the answer follow it down in the order they
  happened. No alternate screen buffer, which would pin a region at the cost of the
  terminal's own scrollback, and that scrollback is TMT's permanent surface. `\033[3J`
  is never sent either: the scrollback above TMT is somebody's shell session and not
  TMT's to throw away.
- **And the prompt box is at the foot of it from the first frame.** Those two look
  like they contradict, and what reconciles them is that the gap between them is
  *inside* the live region, as blank rows above the box — `BottomPad`. `write_above`
  erases the region, prints where it stood, and paints it again below; painting it one
  row shorter puts it back on exactly the rows it already occupied, so the box does not
  move and the printed line lands in the space the pad gave up. The window fills from
  the top while the box holds still, and once the pad is gone the box is at the bottom
  and the terminal's own scrolling keeps it there.

  `opening_pad` is answerable only at the start, because the screen has just been
  cleared and the cursor is on a row we know. After that the count never has to be
  exact: it only ever decreases, it is part of the region's own line list so no repaint
  arithmetic depends on it, and a row out means the box sits a row off the bottom until
  the next line of output corrects it. The last row of the window is never drawn on —
  a region is painted by writing each row and a newline, so the cursor ends one below.
- Repaint in place; never print a second copy of a frame. `LiveRegion` exists for this.
- Blank lines are structure. Two elements with nothing between them are one element.

## Two surfaces, and which one a thing belongs to

This is the most important rule in the file, because getting it wrong is what
produced the defect the response history was built to fix.

Everything TMT draws is either **permanent** or **temporary**, and the two are
rendered by different code that must never be mixed:

| | Permanent | Temporary |
|---|---|---|
| What | progress messages, tool activity, file edits, command and test results, warnings, errors, milestones, the next-step hint, the final answer | the progress bar, the THINKING word, the token count, the elapsed readout, the streaming reply box |
| Written by | `agent_ui.Transcript` | `agent_ui.LiveUI` and `agent_live_renderer.LiveRelay` |
| How | printed once into the terminal's own scrollback | repainted in place through `LiveRegion` |
| Lifetime | the rest of the session | until the next repaint |
| Recorded in | `agent_ui.ResponseHistory`, append-only | nothing |

A temporary row is written with `\r\033[2K` — a carriage return and an erase.
Anything sent there is gone the moment the next thing arrives. That is correct
for a bar measuring progress and wrong for everything else, and for a long
time it was the only surface there was, so every intermediate message erased
the one before it.

The seam between them is `LiveRegion.write_above()`: it erases the live
region, prints the permanent text where the region stood, and paints the
region again below. Paint again rather than shift — how far the terminal
scrolled is something only the terminal knows, and arithmetic that assumed a
fixed offset is what used to march the frame down the screen.

**Never print past a live region.** Either the terminal is yours or it is the
region's; `write_above` is how you borrow it.

## The prominence ladder

Terminals cannot size type independently, so weight is carried by what a
terminal does have: dimness, indentation, the blank line at the edge of a
block, and how loud the marker is. Four levels, in `agent_ui.PROMINENCE`:

| Level | Kinds | Treatment |
|---|---|---|
| 3 | `final` | the boxed reply from `render_response`. One answer per turn, drawn one way. |
| 2 | `file_edit`, `file_create`, `file_delete`, `command`, `test` | indented three columns, gradient marker, measured facts on a dim second row, blank line at the edge of the block |
| 1 | `milestone`, `success`, `warning`, `error`, `tool`, `file_read`, `background_agent` | one row, gradient marker, normal weight |
| 0 | `progress`, `next_step_suggestion` | dim throughout, one column of indent, no blank line |

Level 0 must read as something said in passing and level 2 as a result worth
scrolling back to. Check it with ANSI stripped: the indent still separates
them, because colour is confirmation and never the message.

## Where an event sits in the gradient

Position carries the meaning here exactly as it does on the progress bar, and
these are fixed positions rather than the ambient cycle — a line printed once
into scrollback is being read, and nothing the user is reading may animate.

| Position | Kinds |
|---|---|
| 10 (red) | `error` |
| 40 (orange) | `milestone`, `tool`, `file_read`, `command`, `background_agent` |
| 60 (amber) | `warning` |
| 80 (lime) | `file_edit`, `file_create`, `file_delete`, `test` |
| 95 (green) | `success` |
| the one neutral | `progress`, `next_step_suggestion` |

No event gets a colour of its own. A new kind takes a position on this scale.

## Facts on an event

Only measured ones. `+18 -4` means eighteen lines arrived and four left, and
it is written only where both halves are actually known — a `patch_file` knows
both, an `append_file` knows both, a `write_file` knows only what it wrote
because the content it replaced is gone by the time the event is made. An
event with nothing certain to report gets no second row rather than a
plausible-looking zero.

The same rule governs whether an action succeeded. A result is read for
success or failure only when it is the action's own report; a program's output
and a file's contents are data. Scanning those for words like "failed" once
labelled a fully green test run a failure, which is a lie told confidently.

## The running session, top to bottom

Idle, the moment a session opens:

    ████████ ███    ███ ████████           <- drawn once, when the session opens
       ██    ████  ████    ██
       ██    ██ ████ ██    ██
       ██    ██  ██  ██    ██
       ██    ██      ██    ██
      date
      workspace

                          clock · provider · model    <- the caption, dim
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────    <- the prompt box
    > the hint in dim, or the line being typed
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────

Working, after a question has been asked:

                          clock · provider · model    <- permanent: the question,
    > the question as it was typed                       written when it is answered

    · progress                             <- permanent, into scrollback
      ▸ Patched file: config.py
        +3 -2
    · progress
    ┌──────────────────────────┐           <- the reply as it arrives   ┐
    │ ...                      │                                        │ one live
    └──────────────────────────┘                                        │ region,
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────  <- the box, inert   │ at the
    > Working. Ctrl-C to stop.                                          │ foot of
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────                      │ the
    ███████░░░ 72%  ✳ thinking…  (18s · ↓ 142 tokens)                   ┘ window

**Settled facts in the header, moving ones on the box.** The date and the
workspace were decided before the first request and cannot change while the
loop runs, so they are printed once — repeating them per turn only pushed the
conversation up the screen. The clock, the provider and the model can all be
different from one question to the next, so they are stated on the box, which
is drawn again for every question anyway. Each fact appears once.

### One wordmark, three sizes, descending in the order you meet them

`agent_menu.LOGO` is the canonical letterform and there is exactly one of it.
Everything that signs the screen derives from it rather than restating it, so
there is no way for TMT to end up with two wordmarks that are nearly the same:

| Where | Size | Phase |
|---|---|---|
| the launch screen (`agent_splash`) | the block, scaled ×2 | the ambient cycle while waiting or working; fixed `LOGO_PHASE` once settled |
| the startup menu banner | the block | the ambient cycle |
| the session header | the block | fixed, `BRAND_PHASE` |
| any window too narrow or too short for the block | the plain word | fixed |

**What is printed once takes a fixed phase; what is repainted and watched
animates.** The session header is written into scrollback and never redrawn, so
it cannot animate at all and takes `BRAND_PHASE`. The menu's banner and the
launch screen both live in a live region somebody is looking at, and both ride
the cycle — the launch screen is an attract screen, nothing on it is being read
yet, and the wordmark is the natural thing to say the program is alive with.

**The launch screen stops once a state has settled**, wordmark and subtitle
together. That half is mechanical as well as tasteful: "Up to date." is a fact
and a fact that pulses looks like it is still deciding, and a frame that stops
changing is a frame `LiveRegion` skips repainting. The cost usually argued
against an animating logo — that every repaint rewrites every row — is not paid
on this screen, because the subtitle was already oscillating and a region
repaints every row whenever any row differs.

The session header spends five rows on the block only when the window has
room: `HEADER_LOGO_MIN_ROWS` (18) as well as the width. Below either it falls
back to `TMT · date` on one row — on a window that size the rows are worth
more than the size of the letters.

**The bottom of the screen is one region.** The reply, the box and the status
row are painted together by `LiveRelay`, so they keep their order and their
distance from the foot of the window however much permanent output scrolls
past above them. The status row is last because it is the instrument, and an
instrument belongs under the thing it is measuring. It stops when the turn
does: a bar that is not measuring anything and a clock that is not counting
are decoration pretending to be instruments.

**The box is a live region, not a record.** It is taken down the moment it is
answered, and the question is written into scrollback by `render_task` — the
caption, and the line under the same marker it was typed under, with no rules.
Left behind instead, every question of the session would sit on screen inside
a frame that no longer accepts anything, and an empty prompt would stack an
empty box each time it was answered.

## The meter

`+1231 lines, -123 lines, ~15k tokens in, 30k out` — what the session has changed and
what it has cost. It rides on the **left end of the prompt box's caption**, opposite the
clock, and on the running box it has that row to itself.

**Nothing is drawn outside the flow of the screen.** The meter was briefly pinned to row
one, held there by narrowing the terminal's scrolling region to rows two and below. That
worked, and the cost was the whole session: lines scrolled out of a narrowed region are
discarded rather than pushed into the terminal's scrollback, so the history stopped
accumulating, scrolling up no longer reached it, and the box jumped about when it was
scrolled back. TMT's permanent surface **is** that scrollback — it is the only record a
finished session leaves — and nothing may be bought with it, least of all a readout.
Neither DECSTBM nor the alternate screen buffer has any business here, and a test says so.

- **Only the two counts are coloured**, and each takes the position it already holds:
  green (95) for lines that arrived, red (10) for lines that left. Everything else is
  the one neutral, and the whole row reads with the escapes stripped.
- **A guessed number is marked guessed.** Tokens sent carry `~` always — no provider
  will count a request before it is sent. Tokens generated carry it while the reply is
  still arriving, and when the provider reported no figure of its own. Lines are
  counted, from the events the actions themselves reported, and only where both halves
  are known — which is why a `write_file` says `Created file:` for a path that did not
  exist and `Wrote file:` for one that did.
- **It moves as the work happens**, not when the turn ends: `note_output` feeds it the
  characters as they stream, and `count_event` counts each action as it completes. A
  readout that only settles at the end of a turn is a readout nobody watches.
- **Nothing to report means nothing drawn**, not a row of zeroes.
- It gives up its words before its figures as the terminal narrows, gives up the whole
  meter before any fact on the right end, and says nothing at all before it would show
  a fragment of a number.

## The prompt box

- A dim caption, a rule, a `>` marker and the line, a rule. `-` and `>` when the
  stream cannot encode the rule. Plain, not gradient: see the table above.
- It is the primary surface of the screen — the only bordered thing below the
  answer, and the only one with a live caret in it. That is prominence enough
  without decoration.
- A blank line above it, always. It arrives under the header or under the
  answer's border, and butted against either it reads as part of that rather
  than as the next question.
- The line scrolls sideways rather than wrapping, and both the offset and the
  caret are measured in columns. An offset counted in characters puts a wide
  one half off the edge.
- **The caret stays in the input row.** It is the terminal's own, and it is
  taken out of the row only for a repaint that has something new to show —
  never on a timer. `LiveRegion` writes the repaint with the caret suppressed
  and hands it back once it is in the row again, so it is never drawn on a row
  it is only passing through. Getting either half of this wrong is what put a
  caret at the foot of the terminal flickering twelve times a second while
  nobody was typing.
- Movement is by grapheme. A cursor that steps one code point lands inside a
  combining sequence and everything drawn from there is cut through a
  character.
- The terminal is measured per paint, never remembered: a window resized
  between two keystrokes is drawn at its new width.
- **While a turn runs the box is inert**, drawn by the relay with
  `running_lines`: no caret, no caption, and shadow text saying what is
  happening and how to stop it. Nothing typed into it would be read, so a box
  that looked ready for input would be a lie about what the program is doing.
- **Any doubt about raw keys means no.** A wrong "yes" hangs the process on a
  read that will never be answered; a wrong "no" only degrades to reading a
  whole line. Every scripted, piped and tested run takes the degraded path, and
  that is why they work at all. That path prints the box rather than painting
  it, and does not take it down: there is no repaint, so the box is the record.

## The system prompt

The model never speaks to the user. It emits one JSON object, and what the user
reads is inside `"message"`. The prompt's whole job is to make that unmissable,
and it used to open by undermining it — "You chat naturally with the user" — which
is exactly what a capable model then did. Three things carry it now:

- **The header states the contract first**, before any rule: the reply goes to a
  parser, prose outside the object reaches nobody, and being conversational
  happens *inside* `message`. A greeting, a refusal and a question back are all
  named as respond actions, because those are the three cases a model reaches for
  prose in.
- **`ANSWERING_EXAMPLES` shows it**, for every situation the model will really be
  in — small talk, a question it can answer, a question needing a read first, a
  change, a failure, a refusal, nothing to do, needing the user, and a follow-up
  that depends on an earlier turn. Each is "they said / you emit", with the exact
  object. A model that reads nothing else copies the nearest example, so every
  example is valid JSON, a valid action, and obeys every rule stated elsewhere —
  there is a test that says so.
- **A `WHAT NEVER WORKS` list**, because the failures are specific and repeated:
  a fence, a preamble, prose after the object, thinking-out-loud before it.

Every task ends with a respond whose message summarises what was made. Stated in
the header, in the output rules, and again in BEHAVIOUR with a right and a wrong
example — the message is the only thing the user ever sees, so work not described
there did not reach them.

## The next-step hint

**Four words is the ask; six is the ceiling.** `TARGET_SUGGESTION_WORDS` is what
the prompt demands and what every example in it obeys; `MAX_SUGGESTION_WORDS` is
what `validate_suggestion` lets through. The gap is deliberate. A single limit
that was also the truncation point meant every overshoot came out cut mid-phrase,
and "Run the integration tests for" reads worse than the five-word line it was.
So the ask is strict and the ceiling above it is slack: aim at four, let a five or
a six arrive whole, and cut only at seven, where it has stopped being a hint.

Counted the way a reader counts them: punctuation is not a word, a hyphenated
form is one. It is a bare imperative, never a sentence, an offer or a question —
there is one line of shadow text beside a cursor and no room for one.

**It is shadow text in the next prompt box and nothing else.** It is recorded
in the history — a hint that was offered was offered — and `Transcript` draws
no line for it, exactly as it draws none for the final answer, and for the same
reason: something else on screen already is it. It used to be printed as a
lead-in above the answer as well, which put the same five words on screen twice
a few rows apart in two different styles, and announced inside the reply a line
the reader was about to see under their own cursor.

The input buffer is empty; the hint is drawn, never assigned. The first
character typed replaces it in the same keystroke, so it can never be submitted
by accident and can never end up joined to what the user typed.

When the model offers none, the substitute is read off the turn's own history
rather than invented. A turn that ran no tests is never told to go and look at
test results. The first question of a session has no turn to read, and gets
`agent_ui.OPENING_SUGGESTION` — measured by the same five-word rule, so the
opening line and every line after it read as the same kind of thing.

## The session, and what one question carries from the last

`agent_session.Session` is the one record of a running session: the provider,
the model, the workspace and the conversation. The first three are properties
that answer from the modules that own them, so nothing here can be a stale copy
of a model Settings has since moved. The fourth is the only thing it holds.

- **TMT's own shape, never a provider's.** The conversation is a list of
  `{"role", "content"}`, and `Provider.convert_messages` turns it into whatever
  the active adapter needs. That is what lets the model or the provider change
  mid-session without the history having to be rebuilt or thrown away.
- **The turn, not the transcript.** What is carried forward is each question,
  its answer, and what the turn measurably did — the files it changed and the
  milestones it reached, read off the events. "Now add percentage support" is a
  sentence about a file and the file is the part the answer most often leaves
  out; a turn that only committed and pushed says so nowhere else at all. The
  tool calls in between are not carried; they belong to the turn that made them.
- **Every turn is recorded, however it ended.** It used to take an answer to be
  recorded at all, so a stream failure, a circuit break, an unreadable reply or a
  turn that ran out of steps dropped the user's *question* with it — and the next
  question arrived with no sign the exchange had happened. That is exactly what
  "it has no context between prompts" looked like from outside. A turn with no
  answer carries its `outcome` instead, said by TMT on the question rather than
  as words the model never wrote.
- **A machine's error is never recorded as the model's answer.** Every failure in
  `agent_model` has to come back as a valid action, because the loop has no other
  shape to receive one in — so they carry `SYNTHETIC_KEY`, and `is_synthetic` is
  how the loop tells "the model said this" from "TMT is reporting a failure".
  Without it the next turn was told the model had said "no JSON object found in
  response". Prose where JSON was asked for is the opposite case: those are the
  model's own words, and they are passed through as the answer rather than
  thrown away for an error string that tells the user nothing.
- **The carried answer is the JSON action the model speaks in.** Every other
  assistant message in a request is a JSON object; dropping the last turn's
  answer in as loose prose put an example of the forbidden shape in front of the
  model, in its own voice, immediately before asking it not to use that shape.
  The words are the model's own either way — only the wrapper is restored.
- **Bounded by the model's real window.** `context_window()` reads the
  catalogue entry for the model actually selected; `CONTEXT_SHARE` is how much
  of it the history may take, because the system prompt goes in front of the
  history and the turn's own results pile up behind it. A model with no entry
  gets a small fallback: an unknown limit is a reason to send less.
- **Dropped, never summarised.** A summary of a turn is a claim about what
  happened in it, and nothing here can make one that is true. Oldest first, and
  the newest turn always survives.
- **It dies with the process.** Nothing in `agent_session.py` opens a file. A
  task abandoned on Tuesday must not turn up as context on Wednesday.
- `trim_messages(messages, pinned)` may never trim into the pinned head — the
  system prompt, the carried turns, and the task. Trimming the question out of
  a request leaves the model answering something nobody asked.

## Things not to do

- Do not add a colour to distinguish a new feature. Use position in the existing
  gradient.
- Do not centre body text. Centre the logo, left-align everything readable.
- Do not draw a box around something that is already obviously grouped.
- Do not draw a second rule where one already marks the boundary.
- Do not animate anything the user is trying to read — and a surface with a
  caret in it is being read.
- Do not fix a caret problem with extra newlines, a permanently hidden cursor,
  or a full redraw. The cause is always a cursor move that was drawn; suppress
  the drawing, not the symptom.
